    "description": "Iran’s provincial courts of appeal review eligible judgments within a statutory system of civil and criminal remedies. Their role differs from Supreme Court cassation, and legislation determines which decisions follow each route.",
    "tags": ["Appeals", "Civil procedure", "Criminal procedure", "Provincial courts"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T23:34:54Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T23:34:54Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Courts of Appeal in Iran: Provincial Judicial Review",
      "description": "How Iran’s provincial appeals courts review civil and criminal judgments, and how their role differs from Supreme Court cassation."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "provincial-review",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A provincial route of review",
        "content": "Iran’s provincial court of appeal is known as the dadgah-e tajdidnazar-e ostan. In criminal proceedings, Article 426 identifies it as the reviewing forum for non-final judgments except matters assigned to the Supreme Court. The court is established in the provincial capital. [s1]\n\nThis allocation prevents a misleading picture of an automatic ladder from trial court to provincial appeal and then Supreme Court. Some criminal judgments are final under statutory exceptions; specified serious cases follow the Supreme Court cassation route instead. Eligibility depends on the legal classification of the decision and offence. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "civil-review",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "What civil appellate review examines",
        "content": "Civil appeal can address the reliability of evidence, the legal qualifications of witnesses, failure to consider submitted evidence, jurisdiction, and incompatibility with statutory or religious legal standards. The reviewing court is generally confined to matters challenged on appeal that were decided at first instance. [s2]\n\nThe remedy depends on the defect. A jurisdictional error can lead to transfer to the competent forum. Reversal of a procedural dismissal may require return for consideration of the substance. Where a challenge to a judgment succeeds, the appellate court can reverse it and issue the appropriate decision. These differences matter: “appeal allowed” does not always describe the same procedural outcome. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "criminal-and-special-rules",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Criminal review and special jurisdictions",
        "content": "Criminal appeal grounds include challenges to the evidentiary basis, legality, jurisdiction, and consideration of defence evidence. Article 455 provides for confirmation of a lawful, evidentially supported judgment and for reversal where acquittal is warranted. These powers allow appellate scrutiny of substantive defects as well as procedural ones. [s1]\n\nPeace-court decisions have their own appeal provisions. The Dispute Resolution Councils Law identifies particular decisions that can be challenged before the provincial court of appeal; it does not make every peace-court ruling appealable. The same legislation also assigns specified jurisdictional disputes involving peace courts to provincial appellate courts. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "review-quality",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A remedy on paper and a remedy in practice",
        "content": "The existence of another judicial tier does not establish the quality of review in an individual case. Human Rights Watch’s account of events in 2025 described convictions following grossly unfair proceedings and failures to investigate torture allegations. It provides a reason to assess access to counsel, treatment of evidence, and judicial reasoning rather than infer effectiveness from institutional structure alone. [s4]\n\nFor readers comparing judicial systems, the central distinction is between the availability of a legal remedy and evidence that it corrected an error. This page establishes the former; it does not supply a measured appellate success rate.",
        "source_ids": ["s4"]
      }
    ],
